{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>The carved images of their elohim you shall burn with fire; you shall not desire the silver and gold upon them, nor take it for yourself.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory said in tractate Avodah Zarah: ״פסלו לאלוה לא תחמד״ — if you can nullify it as an elohah, you shall not desire it; ״פסלו מאלוה ולקחת לך״ — if you have nullified it from being an elohah, you may take it for yourself. The sense is that the word פסילים carries two meanings: one, the sense of פסל and actual idolatry; the other, the sense of nullification, from the root פסלות. And so Rashi of blessed memory explained in his commentaries. Likewise the language of the verse bears two senses: one for prohibition and one for permission — one for prohibition, this is ״לא תחמד״; one for permission, this is ״ולקחת לך״, since an idolatrous object that a gentile has nullified is permitted."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:46:37.186081553Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}